WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:19.932
LG: I'm Larry Goldstein, I'm a professor of cellular and molecular medicine

00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:24.266
at UC San Diego, and I'm pleased to be here interviewing Paul Berg

00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:29.699
in a second segment, about Paul's experiences both originally

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.966
in the regulation of recombinant DNA as you heard

00:00:32.967 --> 00:00:35.632
in the previous segment, and now I want to move him along

00:00:35.633 --> 00:00:41.666
to discussions of places where the original model of recombinant DNA

00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:43.866
has not worked as well, where the issues have perhaps

00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:46.432
been a little more socially complicated. Paul, you'll remember,

00:00:46.433 --> 00:00:50.899
is a Nobel Laureate, a winner of the Public Policy Award

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:54.932
of the American Society for Cell Biology, and in some ways a role model

00:00:54.933 --> 00:00:57.899
that many of us have sought to emulate in our dealings with

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.499
the body politic and the press and everything else, so Paul

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:05.266
thank you for joining us again. PB: Thank you. LG: So thinking about

00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:10.032
Asilomar and how Asilomar and recombinant DNA regulation

00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:13.832
really set the tone for scientists calling for regulation

00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:17.632
and driving the kind of regulation that has in fact developed

00:01:17.633 --> 00:01:22.432
and been followed. I know that genetically modified foods and the stem cell

00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:26.366
debates have not lent themselves very well to that kind of model.

00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:30.899
Are there principles you think or reasons why that has not worked so well

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:35.366
in this context? PB: Yes, so let me go back a little bit. The scientists

00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:39.766
did not call for regulation. The Asilomar meeting was actually the

00:01:39.767 --> 00:01:44.699
attempt to bring out the various views and various constituencies

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.332
and to try to assess, was there or was there not a risk.

00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:53.766
The assessment was, it was some risk, but we could mitigate those risks

00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:57.266
by having these guidelines. So the guidelines were a recommendation

00:01:57.267 --> 00:02:03.299
of how to proceed. Now if you look at some of the other issues

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.966
that have confronted science and public policy, the one that

00:02:07.967 --> 00:02:12.366
came shortly thereafter was genetically modified foods.

00:02:12.367 --> 00:02:15.866
And the question was, there's lots of data that says it's safe

00:02:15.867 --> 00:02:19.999
and there's lots of public policy that doesn't want to have it in their backyard.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.666
Would an Asilomar-type meeting help to bring together these

00:02:25.667 --> 00:02:29.866
two opposing views and try to draw some kind of consensus

00:02:29.867 --> 00:02:35.699
and the answer that we found was, they were almost irreconcilable views

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.399
and the views on the opposition were mostly cultural.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:46.632
They weren't questioning the science in large part. And in the main

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:51.132
in Europe particularly, it was the desire to try and maintain the traditional

00:02:51.133 --> 00:02:56.032
type of agriculture. So we just never could get the two sides to mesh

00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:59.999
LG: Even on safety issues? Cause I know the opponents often claim

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.666
that there are safety problems. PB: Right, so the companies set up trials,

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:08.266
they did all kinds of, the FDA approved many of these things after examining

00:03:08.267 --> 00:03:13.232
the data. There was always the problem of the dissemination of weeds

00:03:13.233 --> 00:03:19.866
that would take hold. All these kinds of things arose and slowly but surely,

00:03:19.867 --> 00:03:26.032
the scientific data dispelled almost all of them, but never could challenge

00:03:26.033 --> 00:03:30.799
people at the core values of their culture. What they felt about food,

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:34.499
what food meant to them. And in many ways one of the problems

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:40.032
I think early on, and the agriculture business recognized it is, all the genetic

00:03:40.033 --> 00:03:43.832
modifications they were making were to facilitate better yields for the farmers,

00:03:43.833 --> 00:03:47.932
make work easier for the farmers. The public would say, well what do

00:03:47.933 --> 00:03:53.532
I get out of it? I'm taking the risk of eating something that you tell me is safe,

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:57.799
but may turn out not to be safe in the long run. So why should I take

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:04.366
that risk when all it's doing is making the farmers' job easier and richer.

00:04:04.367 --> 00:04:07.666
LG: So was this a case, maybe, of the scientific community not finding

00:04:07.667 --> 00:04:11.632
the right way to explain or the right message or the right way to reach people?

00:04:11.633 --> 00:04:15.466
PB: Well, I'm not sure what kind of message you could do, cause it's,

00:04:15.467 --> 00:04:17.999
we'll get to in a moment...LG: Well you can tell people that it would make

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.966
food less expensive for them or more nutritious. PB: All of those arguments

00:04:20.967 --> 00:04:25.032
were made. All of those... LG: And they didn't work? PB: No. And my own view

00:04:25.033 --> 00:04:30.799
thought, ultimately a shoppers going to walk into a market and see two bins

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.332
and there's the bin that was genetically modified and there's the wild type

00:04:35.333 --> 00:04:39.466
and the price is going to be half for the genetically modified

00:04:39.467 --> 00:04:44.032
and they are going to have to make a choice, and the pocketbook

00:04:44.033 --> 00:04:48.399
again will be... LG: Free markets sometimes correct these things.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.299
PB: Now, the issue of culture is the same thing with the stem cell issue.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:58.866
So I don't how many meetings when the embryonic stem cell issue arose,

00:04:58.867 --> 00:05:04.666
there was the violent opposition coming from essentially

00:05:04.667 --> 00:05:10.132
the cultural right wing, but religious organizations, and then people

00:05:10.133 --> 00:05:16.066
who themselves felt deeply about the sanctity of life and the anti-abortion

00:05:16.067 --> 00:05:23.632
constituency. So everything was opposed to the embryonic stem cell work.

00:05:23.633 --> 00:05:28.332
I don't know how many meetings I attended, where these

00:05:28.333 --> 00:05:33.766
two constituencies met, and it was like talking by each other.

00:05:33.767 --> 00:05:38.966
Talking right past each other, you could not persuade those who felt

00:05:38.967 --> 00:05:44.999
deeply and conscientiously about sanctity of life, that you couldn't

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:50.099
convince them that this little blastocyst was not a definition of life.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.699
And that is not so well understood when people want to say,

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.132
well let's have a conference and bring these two opposing views together.

00:05:57.133 --> 00:06:01.732
They have to be views that are willing to listen to each other, and

00:06:01.733 --> 00:06:06.832
that there is some give-and-take as there was in the recombinant DNA

00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:09.566
and that some of these other things there is no middle ground.

00:06:09.567 --> 00:06:13.632
LG: Well of course, with embryonic stem cells, Congress got into the act very quickly

00:06:13.633 --> 00:06:16.899
in this case didn't they? PB: Yes. LG: So you testified I know in a

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:20.499
number of hearings in the Senate. Were you in one of the first ones?

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:28.066
PB: I was, I don't know, in any number of them, yes, and what you could see,

00:06:28.067 --> 00:06:33.966
what was being played out was this cultural, religious perspective

00:06:33.967 --> 00:06:41.366
against the scientific opportunities. And it was easy for that other side to

00:06:41.367 --> 00:06:45.166
pooh-pooh the scientific opportunities, and in fact say, well you haven't

00:06:45.167 --> 00:06:49.299
done it yet. And you'd argue, well we can't do it yet, because you won't

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:56.599
allow us to do it. I think if I look back on that whole period of

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:01.466
the scientific community coming together, at least California, and being

00:07:01.467 --> 00:07:06.899
able to get embryonic stem cell work approved, making the case to the public

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:11.799
and getting an almost overwhelming approval, is another example

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:19.966
where the scientific community used its reputation for, and I'm going to go

00:07:19.967 --> 00:07:25.366
back and say Asilomar set a tone. Learn to trust the scientists.

00:07:25.367 --> 00:07:31.432
My engagement in that public policy debate wasn't as rewarding as

00:07:31.433 --> 00:07:37.699
the recombinant DNA was, but I'll tell you, I'm just going to admit to something

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:42.332
which I have admitted to a number of people, during that entire debate

00:07:42.333 --> 00:07:49.966
I along with most of us said adult stem cells were never going to be useful.

00:07:49.967 --> 00:07:56.166
We have to rely on embryonic stem cells. And I think today there's

00:07:56.167 --> 00:08:00.066
more work going on, and more interesting things going on with adult stem cells.

00:08:00.067 --> 00:08:02.366
LG: Well there's terrific work going on with both, right? PB: With both.

00:08:02.367 --> 00:08:08.732
But I think what I found is dismissing the utility of adult stem cells,

00:08:08.733 --> 00:08:14.032
but in fact more and more of them are being cultured and propagated

00:08:14.033 --> 00:08:20.266
and maintained and in terms of the debate about embryo versus

00:08:20.267 --> 00:08:26.632
adult, I think the most immediate benefits are going to come out of either

00:08:26.633 --> 00:08:34.666
adult somatic stem cells from various tissues, or from the iPS cells

00:08:34.667 --> 00:08:38.799
the cells that were adult cells that have been converted

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:46.366
to embryonic-like cells. And I think the debate was good to have,

00:08:46.367 --> 00:08:52.199
and in the end I think we have progress, which is important.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:56.566
LG: There's good science happening, which is what really matters on all fronts.

00:08:56.567 --> 00:09:00.199
PB: Now just to take the lesson from Asilomar one step further,

00:09:00.200 --> 00:09:04.066
are there going to be other problems that are going to arise?

00:09:04.067 --> 00:09:06.799
LG: Well that is what I was going to ask you. I mean looking forward

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:11.766
doesn't all new technology bring new ethical challenges? PB: More and more

00:09:11.767 --> 00:09:17.366
the big one that is going on now is synthetic biology. Now you can make

00:09:17.367 --> 00:09:22.099
organisms, make anything, and I have defended that and actually

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:26.566
have been involved with things, and I am a strong advocate, but there are

00:09:26.567 --> 00:09:31.866
people saying, "Yes, but...", and the "Yes, but..." is what are the nasty

00:09:31.867 --> 00:09:35.066
things you can do with the same technology, and I think we are going

00:09:35.067 --> 00:09:38.366
to have to work through that, so the government again has set up a

00:09:38.367 --> 00:09:44.632
very strong panel to oversee the developments of synthetic biology

00:09:44.633 --> 00:09:48.299
and several interesting things have developed. So for example,

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:52.866
these commercial places that will make DNA for you, now have a rigorous

00:09:52.867 --> 00:09:57.766
protocol that they have to examine. A request to make a particular

00:09:57.767 --> 00:10:02.866
DNA sequence. They have to translate it and see what it in fact will,

00:10:02.867 --> 00:10:08.999
what kind of protein it will make. Who's going to use it. There's a lot of things

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.432
before they will actually make it and approve it. So the idea that some

00:10:13.433 --> 00:10:16.832
weirdo is going to send in a request for some nasty toxins...

00:10:16.833 --> 00:10:20.632
LG: small pox or whatever is always the nightmare scenario...

00:10:20.633 --> 00:10:21.799
PB: is unlikely to be done.

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:28.732
But nevertheless, synthetic biology is confronting an issue of creating

00:10:28.733 --> 00:10:34.866
microbes that will be making biofuels, and I'm absolutely

00:10:34.867 --> 00:10:39.499
astonished and amazed about what people are actually doing.

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:44.566
I mean Craig Venter is making whole chromosomes, Jay Keasling is

00:10:44.567 --> 00:10:52.266
turning out organisms that are using biomass, the organism programmed

00:10:52.267 --> 00:10:58.366
to actually digest cellulases and xyluloses and convert them into sugar

00:10:58.367 --> 00:11:04.832
internally and make long chain fatty acids that are being used to drive trucks.

00:11:04.833 --> 00:11:08.566
LG: So you sound very optimistic that we can both solve the technical

00:11:08.567 --> 00:11:14.632
as well as the safety concerns there. PB: I think the genome project has

00:11:14.633 --> 00:11:20.766
provided an extraordinary library of information of genes

00:11:20.767 --> 00:11:24.499
LG: From not just humans, but from all organisms. PB: All organisms.

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:31.232
So if you go to an organism, the sweet wormwood plant, and take out three genes

00:11:31.233 --> 00:11:34.232
and put them in E. coli so they make now artemisinin.

00:11:34.233 --> 00:11:38.466
LG: What the heck is artemisinin PB: Artemisinin is the major drug

00:11:38.467 --> 00:11:41.832
now being used for treatment of drug resistant malaria.

00:11:41.833 --> 00:11:44.999
And so it's a very complicated

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:49.632
molecule, and so it's costly to synthesize with the usual procedures.

00:11:49.633 --> 00:11:57.366
Isolating it from the plant is very complicated. So Jay pulled out the three genes

00:11:57.367 --> 00:12:01.332
that carry out the last three steps of the synthesis of artemisinin,

00:12:01.333 --> 00:12:06.466
engineered the steroid pathway of E. coli. So you make a precursor

00:12:06.467 --> 00:12:11.866
and with the three enzymes from the wormwood plant they pour out

00:12:11.867 --> 00:12:16.699
artemisinin and now big plant manufacturing it on a commercial scale.

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:21.966
LG: So you make an anti-malaria drug in an E. coli fermenter. Incredible.

00:12:21.967 --> 00:12:28.266
PB: And that is now making kerosenes, making airplane fuels.

00:12:28.267 --> 00:12:35.299
LG: So in retrospect you took an enormous leap when you walked out of the lab

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:40.632
and decided to spend some time working with the public.

00:12:40.633 --> 00:12:44.432
Is that a good model going forward? Do we need more of that? Do we need

00:12:44.433 --> 00:12:48.266
less of it? What are your thoughts about that? PB: Well there are people

00:12:48.267 --> 00:12:56.966
who can do this. Who are articulate and persuasive and could play a role,

00:12:56.967 --> 00:13:01.566
and there are those that can't and won't. And I think we have to encourage

00:13:01.567 --> 00:13:07.232
people because, after all, this is our world, the scientific community is

00:13:07.233 --> 00:13:13.632
and what we do is our world. And if we don't defend it, in one way, explain it

00:13:13.633 --> 00:13:18.032
then we are not doing half the job. We publish papers

00:13:18.033 --> 00:13:22.332
as a way of communicating what we've done, LG: To each other.

00:13:22.333 --> 00:13:26.566
PB: But the general public doesn't read these papers and they are in the dark.

00:13:26.567 --> 00:13:30.832
And you are using mumbo-jumbo to describe what you do.

00:13:30.833 --> 00:13:38.332
And so there are people who are very skilled and enjoy actually explaining

00:13:38.333 --> 00:13:42.166
what they do in more general terms to the general public

00:13:42.167 --> 00:13:48.899
and that's a very valuable thing for those people to do it. I think we have to

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:54.466
clear the path so it's not sacrificing ones career. LG: Yeah, I mean

00:13:54.467 --> 00:13:57.232
if one were to make the argument to some people and say, well

00:13:57.233 --> 00:14:00.199
you can only do it after you've gotten the Nobel Prize, which means it's

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:03.599
a very small population. PB: Well, okay, it doesn't have to be a Nobel Prize,

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:08.699
it could be when your career has gelled to the point where you're now

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:12.432
recognized so that in fact if you are going to make statements

00:14:12.433 --> 00:14:16.799
people will say it is coming from authoritative source. LG: Build some respect.

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:22.232
PB: Younger people sometimes say, nuh-uh I don't want to get involved in that

00:14:22.233 --> 00:14:27.266
I'm in science to do experiments, but when you grow up, okay,

00:14:27.267 --> 00:14:33.099
and it is when you grow up and you feel assured of a modicum of success

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:38.799
that you can go out and do this, and you have to do it conscientiously

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:41.799
and seriously and honestly. LG: And you have to bring rigorous science.

00:14:41.800 --> 01:00:00.000
PB: And you have to bring rigorous science. LG: Thank you. PB: Good.

